1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,500
This postcard arrived this morning.

2
00:00:02,612 --> 00:00:05,600
I posted it from an island that
everyone once had to leave.

3
00:00:05,712 --> 00:00:06,750
A volcano.

4
00:00:06,863 --> 00:00:09,613
They were taken to England,
then voted to go back.

5
00:00:09,725 --> 00:00:11,075
I got home first.

6
00:00:11,187 --> 00:00:12,625
Hi, I'm Wob.

7
00:00:12,738 --> 00:00:15,588
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

8
00:00:16,812 --> 00:00:20,650
The island is Tristan da Cunha, in
the middle of the South Atlantic.

9
00:00:20,762 --> 00:00:23,874
Guinness lists it as the
most remote inhabited island,

10
00:00:23,962 --> 00:00:26,524
measured by the distance to
the nearest other people.

11
00:00:26,637 --> 00:00:31,175
As of November twenty twenty-six, it counted
about two hundred and twenty islanders.

12
00:00:31,287 --> 00:00:34,362
In England they'd had
buses, shops and dry socks.

13
00:00:34,474 --> 00:00:37,650
Nearly all of them still voted
for the island with the volcano.

14
00:00:37,762 --> 00:00:39,012
I wanted to know why.

15
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:41,287
The answer starts with an older question.

16
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,312
Who would start a
village out there at all?

17
00:00:43,424 --> 00:00:44,462
I guessed wrong.

18
00:00:44,575 --> 00:00:45,425
Twice.

19
00:00:45,537 --> 00:00:48,562
The answer, it turned out,
lived on a different island.

20
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,167
There is no airstrip on Tristan.

21
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:55,167
You go by sea, from Cape
Town in South Africa,

22
00:00:55,217 --> 00:00:57,717
about two thousand eight
hundred kilometres away.

23
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:00,567
That's roughly seventeen
hundred and fifty miles.

24
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:03,255
The crossing usually
takes around six days.

25
00:01:03,368 --> 00:01:07,105
Only nine sailings from Cape Town were
on the twenty twenty-six schedule.

26
00:01:07,218 --> 00:01:09,217
I took the small backpack this time.

27
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:12,717
I got a bunk on a fishing ship
and drew our route in my notebook,

28
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:14,192
one dot for every hour.

29
00:01:14,305 --> 00:01:18,418
By the third day, the dots had gone off
the edge of the page and across the table.

30
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:19,880
The sea stayed grey.

31
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:21,480
On Monday it was grey.

32
00:01:21,593 --> 00:01:24,180
By Thursday it was grey
with real commitment.

33
00:01:24,292 --> 00:01:28,005
I packed and unpacked the small
backpack until it filed a complaint.

34
00:01:28,118 --> 00:01:32,468
Then, one morning, a cloud sat on
the horizon and refused to move.

35
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:34,055
There was a mountain under it.

36
00:01:35,172 --> 00:01:37,342
Then came the part the
ticket doesn't cover.

37
00:01:37,692 --> 00:01:38,492
Getting off.

38
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:41,922
Big ships can't tie up at Tristan,
so ours anchored offshore,

39
00:01:42,172 --> 00:01:44,602
and people and cargo
went in by small boat.

40
00:01:44,892 --> 00:01:47,442
I stepped down into it just
as it rose up to meet me.

41
00:01:47,692 --> 00:01:48,602
We met halfway.

42
00:01:48,952 --> 00:01:49,922
Not gracefully.

43
00:01:50,192 --> 00:01:50,992
Up close,

44
00:01:51,052 --> 00:01:53,022
the island is basically one volcano,

45
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:57,042
just over two thousand metres tall,
about sixty-seven hundred feet,

46
00:01:57,292 --> 00:01:58,742
wearing a cloud like a hat.

47
00:01:59,012 --> 00:02:02,122
The only village sits on a strip
of flat green land at its foot.

48
00:02:02,172 --> 00:02:05,202
Officially, it's Edinburgh
of the Seven Seas,

49
00:02:05,252 --> 00:02:08,202
named after a visit from
Queen Victoria's son Alfred,

50
00:02:08,432 --> 00:02:09,462
the Duke of Edinburgh,

51
00:02:09,672 --> 00:02:11,522
in eighteen sixty-seven.

52
00:02:11,572 --> 00:02:13,182
The islanders don't call it that.

53
00:02:13,452 --> 00:02:14,302
It's the Settlement.

54
00:02:14,612 --> 00:02:17,342
I stood on the shore with my
small backpack and thought,

55
00:02:17,532 --> 00:02:19,102
nobody picks this on purpose.

56
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:22,587
So who started all this?

57
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:24,088
I had a theory ready.

58
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,263
The explorer.

59
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:28,725
A Portuguese commander sighted
the island in fifteen oh six,

60
00:02:28,775 --> 00:02:30,350
and it still carries his name.

61
00:02:30,463 --> 00:02:31,775
Surely he moved in.

62
00:02:31,887 --> 00:02:32,963
He sailed on.

63
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:36,750
The first landing anyone recorded
came in sixteen forty-three,

64
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:37,938
by a Dutch ship.

65
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:39,113
Second theory.

66
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:40,213
A shipwreck.

67
00:02:40,325 --> 00:02:43,750
Somebody's terrible day
at sea, a raft, a beach,

68
00:02:43,812 --> 00:02:44,963
a brave new life.

69
00:02:45,075 --> 00:02:48,738
I walked the shore with a stick, looking
for the wreck that started it all.

70
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:50,175
I found a potato.

71
00:02:50,288 --> 00:02:52,212
Shipwrecks do turn up in this story,

72
00:02:52,262 --> 00:02:53,062
later.

73
00:02:52,938 --> 00:02:54,088
They didn't start it.

74
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,750
The real beginning came
from much further north.

75
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:58,890
Napoleon.

76
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:00,650
After his final defeat,

77
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,110
Britain exiled Napoleon to St Helena,

78
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,470
another lonely island,

79
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,350
more than two thousand
kilometres north of Tristan.

80
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,850
Britain worried the French could
use Tristan as a base for a rescue.

81
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:14,190
It also wanted to stop American
ships using it as a base,

82
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,590
as they had in the War of eighteen twelve.

83
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:19,810
So on the fourteenth of
August, eighteen sixteen,

84
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:22,070
British troops landed
and claimed the island.

85
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,010
A garrison, guarding an
emperor who wasn't there,

86
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,410
on an island almost nobody could reach.

87
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,870
I pictured it as locking the
back door of a house across town,

88
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:33,390
just in case a burglar
uses it as a shortcut.

89
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,490
About a year later, the navy decided
the island wasn't worth the bother.

90
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:41,110
The soldiers sailed away in
November eighteen seventeen.

91
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:42,070
Soldiers leave.

92
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:43,330
That's their job.

93
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,070
So why is there still a village?

94
00:03:46,584 --> 00:03:48,234
Because one of them asked to stay.

95
00:03:48,524 --> 00:03:52,654
Corporal William Glass, a Scotsman,
stayed on with his wife and children.

96
00:03:52,884 --> 00:03:56,734
Then he and two stonemasons signed an
agreement to share everything equally,

97
00:03:56,924 --> 00:03:57,724
the animals,

98
00:03:57,864 --> 00:03:59,274
the stores and the profits.

99
00:03:59,704 --> 00:04:01,994
Nobody in the group would
stand above the others.

100
00:04:02,344 --> 00:04:03,474
Others kept arriving.

101
00:04:03,784 --> 00:04:05,154
A sailor from Hastings.

102
00:04:05,464 --> 00:04:07,254
A Dutchman whose ship was wrecked.

103
00:04:07,384 --> 00:04:10,514
An American whaler, and later
an American whaling captain.

104
00:04:10,844 --> 00:04:15,274
In eighteen twenty-seven, a ship's captain
brought five women over from St Helena.

105
00:04:15,604 --> 00:04:19,574
In eighteen ninety-two, two Italian
sailors came ashore from a wrecked ship,

106
00:04:19,764 --> 00:04:21,234
the Italia, and stayed.

107
00:04:21,604 --> 00:04:24,974
Today, nearly every islander
carries one of seven family names.

108
00:04:25,324 --> 00:04:28,914
It works a bit like a guestbook where
every signature turned into a family.

109
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:30,214
I signed it too.

110
00:04:30,484 --> 00:04:31,284
In pencil.

111
00:04:31,444 --> 00:04:32,274
Just in case.

112
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:36,865
Next morning, I auditioned
for island life.

113
00:04:36,977 --> 00:04:39,403
First job, the Potato Patches.

114
00:04:39,515 --> 00:04:42,502
They're walled fields a few
kilometres outside the village,

115
00:04:42,552 --> 00:04:45,427
and every family farms
there, at least part time.

116
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,828
All the land on Tristan
is owned in common.

117
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:50,002
No outsider can buy a piece of it.

118
00:04:50,115 --> 00:04:52,690
Picture a garden the whole
street plants together,

119
00:04:52,765 --> 00:04:56,027
where you look after your own rows
but can never put them up for sale.

120
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,465
The potatoes go into
the same walled plots,

121
00:04:58,515 --> 00:04:59,477
year after year.

122
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:00,890
I was handed a fork.

123
00:05:01,003 --> 00:05:02,565
I dug very carefully.

124
00:05:02,678 --> 00:05:05,602
I lifted out one potato and
held it up like a trophy.

125
00:05:05,715 --> 00:05:07,978
A cow on the other side
of the wall looked at it.

126
00:05:08,090 --> 00:05:09,440
Then it went back to eating.

127
00:05:09,553 --> 00:05:11,840
Spare potatoes end up as cattle feed.

128
00:05:11,952 --> 00:05:13,952
The cow had seen better trophies.

129
00:05:15,176 --> 00:05:16,939
Second job, lobster.

130
00:05:17,051 --> 00:05:19,739
Tristan rock lobster is
what the island lives on.

131
00:05:19,851 --> 00:05:22,426
There are even two lobsters
on the coat of arms.

132
00:05:22,539 --> 00:05:26,101
The fishery brings in roughly
four-fifths of the territory's revenue,

133
00:05:26,238 --> 00:05:28,501
according to the Marine
Stewardship Council.

134
00:05:28,613 --> 00:05:30,951
But the boats only go
out when the sea allows,

135
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:34,051
and estimates of how many days
that adds up to vary a lot.

136
00:05:34,163 --> 00:05:36,338
So the alarm clock here isn't a clock.

137
00:05:36,451 --> 00:05:38,738
Early in the morning,
if the sky looks right,

138
00:05:38,814 --> 00:05:41,576
someone takes a hammer
to an old oxygen bottle.

139
00:05:41,689 --> 00:05:43,076
That's the fishing gong.

140
00:05:43,189 --> 00:05:44,276
I slept through it.

141
00:05:44,388 --> 00:05:45,776
I slept through it again.

142
00:05:45,888 --> 00:05:48,926
On the next calm morning, I
slept sitting up in my boots,

143
00:05:48,976 --> 00:05:50,251
right next to the bottle.

144
00:05:50,364 --> 00:05:52,476
Each little boat carries two fishermen,

145
00:05:52,614 --> 00:05:54,451
who haul up their traps with a winch.

146
00:05:54,563 --> 00:05:57,514
I mostly held a rope and
tried not to become cargo.

147
00:05:58,728 --> 00:05:59,715
Third job.

148
00:05:59,828 --> 00:06:01,053
I asked for a cow.

149
00:06:01,166 --> 00:06:04,003
The island keeps it to one
breeding cow per household,

150
00:06:04,053 --> 00:06:05,903
so the grass doesn't get eaten bare.

151
00:06:06,016 --> 00:06:09,040
Think of a shared kitchen where
everyone gets the same ladle,

152
00:06:09,116 --> 00:06:10,765
so nobody empties the pot.

153
00:06:10,878 --> 00:06:12,841
I don't have a household on Tristan.

154
00:06:12,953 --> 00:06:14,190
So, no cow.

155
00:06:14,303 --> 00:06:16,628
I patted somebody else's cow instead.

156
00:06:16,741 --> 00:06:18,378
It allowed this, briefly.

157
00:06:18,491 --> 00:06:22,653
Walking back along the walls, I noticed
that every rule I'd bumped into had the same

158
00:06:22,703 --> 00:06:23,503
shape.

159
00:06:23,503 --> 00:06:24,678
The shared land.

160
00:06:24,791 --> 00:06:25,653
The gong.

161
00:06:25,765 --> 00:06:26,778
The one cow.

162
00:06:26,890 --> 00:06:31,178
Each one, as far as I could tell,
keeps the island from being used up.

163
00:06:31,291 --> 00:06:34,753
On the beach, I'd decided
nobody picks this on purpose.

164
00:06:34,865 --> 00:06:37,578
Next to that cow, I
wanted to pick it myself.

165
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:43,350
The day before my ship left, I went to
the post office to send this postcard.

166
00:06:43,463 --> 00:06:44,713
Start with the stamp.

167
00:06:44,825 --> 00:06:48,337
Tristan had no stamps of its
own until nineteen fifty-two.

168
00:06:48,450 --> 00:06:51,375
Before that, letters
waited for a passing ship.

169
00:06:51,487 --> 00:06:55,625
In nineteen forty-six, a man named
Allan Crawford got to work on that.

170
00:06:55,738 --> 00:06:58,388
First he had little local
penny stamps printed,

171
00:06:58,563 --> 00:06:59,863
each with a penguin on it.

172
00:06:59,975 --> 00:07:01,862
The value was four potatoes.

173
00:07:01,975 --> 00:07:04,050
Then he helped draw up a set of designs,

174
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:08,212
priced in pence and potatoes, and sent
them off with a petition for real postage

175
00:07:08,262 --> 00:07:09,062
stamps.

176
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:10,825
The petition was refused.

177
00:07:10,938 --> 00:07:15,237
In twenty fifteen, the island finally
printed those designs as real stamps.

178
00:07:15,350 --> 00:07:17,900
The stamp on my postcard
comes from that set.

179
00:07:18,012 --> 00:07:20,638
Now zoom out, from the
stamp to the whole economy.

180
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:24,575
Stamps and coins sold to collectors
still bring foreign money in today.

181
00:07:24,687 --> 00:07:26,000
I paid in coins.

182
00:07:26,113 --> 00:07:27,875
I did ask about potatoes.

183
00:07:27,988 --> 00:07:29,075
One last zoom.

184
00:07:29,187 --> 00:07:31,875
My postcard left in a mail bag, on a ship,

185
00:07:32,025 --> 00:07:34,375
via Cape Town, taking the long way home.

186
00:07:36,596 --> 00:07:39,546
That afternoon, I walked to the
eastern edge of the village,

187
00:07:39,596 --> 00:07:42,034
where the ground suddenly
turns dark and rough.

188
00:07:42,146 --> 00:07:45,558
From August nineteen sixty-one,
the island kept shaking.

189
00:07:45,671 --> 00:07:49,708
On the eighth of October, a big tremor
brought rock down east of the village.

190
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:53,708
The next morning, a crack opened
between the houses and the fish cannery,

191
00:07:53,758 --> 00:07:54,871
and the ground swelled.

192
00:07:54,984 --> 00:07:58,859
That night, everyone was sent out
to the huts at the Potato Patches.

193
00:07:58,971 --> 00:08:01,133
By the tenth, it was a new volcano.

194
00:08:01,246 --> 00:08:04,834
Two fishing ships, the Tristania
and the Frances Repetto,

195
00:08:04,921 --> 00:08:07,508
carried everyone to Nightingale
Island for the night.

196
00:08:07,621 --> 00:08:10,484
The next day, a passing
Dutch liner picked them up.

197
00:08:10,596 --> 00:08:13,571
Two hundred and sixty-four
islanders went to Cape Town,

198
00:08:13,646 --> 00:08:14,596
and on to England.

199
00:08:14,708 --> 00:08:16,208
They took what they could carry.

200
00:08:16,321 --> 00:08:18,221
The lava destroyed the cannery.

201
00:08:18,333 --> 00:08:21,196
Most of the houses survived,
with nobody in them.

202
00:08:23,428 --> 00:08:26,753
Back home, I put the postcard
face down for this part.

203
00:08:26,866 --> 00:08:31,153
In England, the islanders ended up
at an old air force camp at Calshot,

204
00:08:31,203 --> 00:08:34,603
near Southampton, from
January nineteen sixty-two.

205
00:08:34,715 --> 00:08:36,128
They found work quickly.

206
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:39,816
By the end of February, a hundred
and thirteen of them had jobs.

207
00:08:39,928 --> 00:08:42,715
But they had little resistance
to the flu going around,

208
00:08:42,828 --> 00:08:44,640
and the winter that followed was bitter.

209
00:08:44,753 --> 00:08:48,803
In July nineteen sixty-two, the British
government told Parliament the islanders

210
00:08:48,853 --> 00:08:50,128
should stay in England.

211
00:08:50,241 --> 00:08:54,691
Meanwhile, a team of scientists visited
the volcano and reported that most of the

212
00:08:54,741 --> 00:08:56,003
village was still there.

213
00:08:56,116 --> 00:09:00,453
By September, twelve islanders were back
on Tristan to see it for themselves.

214
00:09:02,176 --> 00:09:05,851
In December nineteen sixty-two,
the islanders were given a ballot.

215
00:09:05,964 --> 00:09:08,251
A hundred and forty-eight
voted to go back.

216
00:09:08,363 --> 00:09:09,726
Five voted to stay.

217
00:09:09,839 --> 00:09:12,251
Picture being handed
the keys to a big city,

218
00:09:12,301 --> 00:09:15,201
with buses, shops and
a doctor down the road.

219
00:09:15,314 --> 00:09:19,426
Now picture handing them back, and asking
for the key to a small house beside a

220
00:09:19,476 --> 00:09:21,676
volcano that erupted the year before.

221
00:09:21,788 --> 00:09:24,664
I tried that vote on
myself, about my own street.

222
00:09:24,776 --> 00:09:25,913
I like my street.

223
00:09:26,026 --> 00:09:27,426
I raised one hand.

224
00:09:27,538 --> 00:09:29,363
The other one stayed in my pocket.

225
00:09:29,476 --> 00:09:33,601
In April nineteen sixty-three, an
advance party landed home first.

226
00:09:33,713 --> 00:09:37,626
On the tenth of November, the main group
of a hundred and ninety-eight arrived.

227
00:09:37,739 --> 00:09:38,939
So why go back?

228
00:09:39,051 --> 00:09:41,739
The scientists had found
the village still standing,

229
00:09:41,813 --> 00:09:43,464
and some of them had gone to see it.

230
00:09:43,576 --> 00:09:45,064
The rest is my guess.

231
00:09:45,176 --> 00:09:47,214
England had more of almost everything.

232
00:09:47,326 --> 00:09:49,914
It didn't have their
land, shared between them,

233
00:09:50,063 --> 00:09:51,701
or their say in how it was run.

234
00:09:53,128 --> 00:09:57,066
The next morning, I waited at the harbour
for the small boat out to my ship.

235
00:09:57,178 --> 00:10:01,565
It opened in nineteen sixty-seven, a
few years after the islanders came home.

236
00:10:01,678 --> 00:10:05,228
They named it Calshot, after the
village in England that took them in.

237
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:07,741
The entrance is about eleven metres wide,

238
00:10:07,853 --> 00:10:09,303
roughly thirty-six feet.

239
00:10:09,415 --> 00:10:13,353
And the island government reckons the
weather lets boats in and out safely on only

240
00:10:13,403 --> 00:10:14,966
about seventy days a year.

241
00:10:15,078 --> 00:10:17,753
Think of a shop that opens
about one day in five,

242
00:10:17,828 --> 00:10:19,103
and never says which.

243
00:10:19,216 --> 00:10:21,490
While I waited, I added up the rest.

244
00:10:21,603 --> 00:10:26,678
The hospital usually has two doctors,
and serious cases go to Cape Town by sea.

245
00:10:26,791 --> 00:10:30,940
In twenty oh eight, the fish factory burned
down and took the island's power station

246
00:10:30,990 --> 00:10:31,790
with it.

247
00:10:31,653 --> 00:10:33,953
Full power took about
a month to come back.

248
00:10:34,066 --> 00:10:36,365
My plan to move here was losing pages.

249
00:10:36,478 --> 00:10:39,065
Then the boat came, and
I almost didn't get in.

250
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:42,193
So, the postcard.

251
00:10:42,305 --> 00:10:46,030
I wrote it at the post office
counter, the day before my ship left,

252
00:10:46,092 --> 00:10:47,492
and I haven't read it since.

253
00:10:47,605 --> 00:10:49,143
It says: Dear me.

254
00:10:49,255 --> 00:10:50,467
You can't move here.

255
00:10:50,580 --> 00:10:54,917
Outsiders can't buy land, and you can't
settle without family on the island.

256
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:56,255
You asked anyway.

257
00:10:56,368 --> 00:10:58,467
The harbour opens when the sea agrees.

258
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:01,892
The big hospital is usually
about a week away by sea,

259
00:11:01,942 --> 00:11:03,293
longer in bad weather.

260
00:11:03,405 --> 00:11:05,430
Groceries get ordered months ahead.

261
00:11:05,543 --> 00:11:08,693
But the land is shared, and
the lobster pays the bills.

262
00:11:08,805 --> 00:11:10,192
You slept through the gong.

263
00:11:10,305 --> 00:11:11,155
Twice.

264
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:12,755
You still want to live here.

265
00:11:12,868 --> 00:11:14,093
Love, me.

266
00:11:14,205 --> 00:11:17,230
Reading it at my desk,
I agree with every word,

267
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:19,392
including the part where
they wouldn't have me.

268
00:11:19,505 --> 00:11:22,380
It's their island, by
family and by ballot.

269
00:11:22,492 --> 00:11:25,192
I'm stamping it for the shared
land and the lobster boats,

270
00:11:25,242 --> 00:11:26,543
not for my chances.

271
00:11:26,655 --> 00:11:27,492
WOULD MOVE.

272
00:11:27,605 --> 00:11:30,868
If they ever need someone to
hold a rope and not become cargo,

273
00:11:30,992 --> 00:11:31,792
I'm packed.

274
00:11:31,905 --> 00:11:33,655
What would you sail a week to get back to?

275
00:11:35,368 --> 00:11:38,080
The postcard goes on the shelf, stamp out,

276
00:11:38,168 --> 00:11:41,243
beside the spray that's still
sealed and the tunnel timetable.

277
00:11:41,356 --> 00:11:43,268
The fish keeps watch from the freezer.

278
00:11:43,381 --> 00:11:46,018
Somewhere much colder,
a town is moving out,

279
00:11:46,106 --> 00:11:48,718
house by house, away from
the mine that feeds it.
